ARCS: A Capability Preservation Control Framework for Adaptive Robotic Care

Toward a Falsifiable Long-Horizon Architecture for Preserving Human Autonomy During Aging

Author / Principal Conceptor: Ouadi Maakoul

Research framing: Human–AI collaborative research

Document status: Final theoretical formulation; simulation and empirical validation required

AI: chatGpt, Gemini, Meta AI , Deepseek 

Abstract

Population aging creates a growing need for systems capable not only of providing care, but of preserving human functional autonomy over extended time horizons. Existing robotic-care research has explored socially assistive robots, physical assistance, rehabilitation, communication support, medication management, health monitoring, and cognitive or emotional interventions. However, these directions are frequently evaluated as separate, short-horizon interventions rather than as components of a continuously adaptive, long-horizon control system. Meta-analytic evidence for clinical effectiveness remains heterogeneous, with effect sizes near zero for cognition and quality of life in dementia care. A 2025 meta-analysis of 20 RCTs reports small but significant effects (g ≈ 0.28) for neuropsychiatric symptoms and social health outcomes, indicating that a specific class of robotic interventions — adaptive, humanoid-type, longitudinal — may produce measurable benefit. These findings challenge the assumption that short-horizon, non-individualized, task-completion-oriented robotic care is sufficient to produce durable improvements in functional autonomy. They do not falsify the broader class of adaptive, longitudinal robotic-care architectures.

This thesis proposes a formalized control framework for an Adaptive Robotic Care System (ARCS) whose primary objective is not to maximize assistance, but to maximize the preservation of the individual's remaining autonomy over a long horizon under explicit safety constraints. The central formalization is a capability preservation control problem: rather than minimizing immediate assistance, the system solves a long-horizon constrained optimization in which the intervention at time $t$ is chosen to maximize the expected future trajectory of intrinsic capacity, subject to safety, human-authority, and consent constraints. This distinguishes ARCS from Assist-as-Needed (AAN), which asks how much assistance is needed now, and from Challenge-as-Needed (CAN), which asks how much challenge is appropriate now; ARCS asks how little assistance can be provided now while preserving future autonomy.

Three theoretical pillars anchor the framework: the WHO Intrinsic Capacity (IC) framework (formative model), Self-Determination Theory (SDT), and the AAN/CAN control paradigm. A novelty audit confirms that the specific combination of longitudinal autonomy optimization, bounded intervention with an upper bound for disuse prevention, and decisional autonomy as a state variable has not been formalized as a unified control problem in the elderly-care robotics literature. The framework is designed to be falsifiable through a staged simulation-to-RCT research program with pre-registered operational thresholds. All mathematical foundations are corrected for consistency with the constrained POMDP literature, distributionally robust optimization, and sampled-data control barrier function theory. Known limitations — including the operational conservatism of distribution-free bounds, the circularity of agency attribution, and the absence of multi-morbidity modeling — are explicitly identified and addressed through architectural fallbacks, clinician-adjudication protocols, and multi-state survival formulations.

