Chapter 3: Theoretical Foundations

3.1 WHO Intrinsic Capacity: Formative Model

The WHO IC framework defines intrinsic capacity as the composite of physical and mental capacities across five domains: cognition, locomotion, sensory, vitality, and psychological. ARCS adopts the formative model: the composite is constituted by its domains, not reflected by them. Consequences:

1. Domain-specific intervention logic is required.
2. No single IC score is used as primary outcome; the vector $\mathbf{A}_t = [P_t, C_t, S_t, D_t]^\top$ is retained.
3. Weights are preference-elicited, not factor-analytically derived.

Mapping to IC subdomains: $P_t \leftrightarrow$ Locomotion + Vitality; $C_t \leftrightarrow$ Cognition; $S_t \leftrightarrow$ Psychological (partial); $D_t \leftrightarrow$ Not covered by IC (theoretical gap addressed by SDT).

3.2 Self-Determination Theory

SDT posits three basic psychological needs — autonomy, competence, and relatedness — whose satisfaction is essential for well-being and intrinsic motivation.

SDT Need ARCS Dimension Interpretation
Competence $P_t, C_t$ Sense of effectiveness in action and thought
Autonomy $D_t$ Volition and self-endorsement in choices
Relatedness $S_t$ Connection and belonging

Normative criterion for intervention selection:

\text{If } \Delta \text{TaskCompletion} > 0 \;\wedge\; \Delta(\text{Competence} + \text{Autonomy} + \text{Relatedness}) < 0, \;\text{the intervention is rejected.}

3.3 AAN and CAN: From Point Control to Long-Horizon Policy

AAN demonstrates real-time modulation of assistance based on tracking error while minimizing robotic assistance. CAN shows that early mobilization requires appropriately challenging patients. Maximal assistance hampers voluntary effort and can induce disuse syndrome. The Minimum Necessary Intervention Principle (MNIP) must be embedded within a long-horizon objective; the immediate MNIP becomes the myopic approximation of the long-horizon policy.

