Chapter 5: The Capability Preservation Control Problem

5.1 Multi-State Hazard-Weighted Survival Functional

A binary community-dwelling indicator is inadequate: an individual may remain community-dwelling while experiencing severe functional decline. We replace it with a multi-state survival model:

\mathcal{S}_{\text{states}} = \{\text{independent}, \text{community-dwelling with assistance}, \text{institutionalized}, \text{deceased}\}

Let $\pi_{jk}(t)$ denote the transition rate from state $j$ to state $k$ at time $t$. The occupancy probability of state $j$ is $S_j(t)$, and the value functional becomes:

\boxed{\; J(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi \left[ \int_0^\infty \sum_{j \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{states}}} \omega_j \, S_j(t) \, e^{-\gamma t} \left( \text{API}_{\text{cap}}(\mathbf{A}_t) - \lambda_I C_I(I_t) - \lambda_R R(\mathbf{X}_t, I_t) \right) dt \right] \;}

where $\omega_j$ are preference-elicited weights over states. This formulation prevents perverse incentives: interventions that preserve community dwelling at the cost of functional autonomy are penalized by the $\text{API}_{\text{cap}}$ term.

5.2 Formal RC-POMDP Formulation

The ARCS control problem is a Recursively-Constrained POMDP (RC-POMDP). C-POMDPs violate Bellman's principle of optimality; RC-POMDPs preserve it and admit deterministic optimal policies.

· State space: $\mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{6+n_M}$
· Action space: $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m$, $I_t = (I_t^{\text{assist}}, I_t^{\text{challenge}}, I_t^{\text{modality}}, I_t^{\text{authority}})$
· Observation space: $\mathcal{O}$
· Transition model: $p(\mathbf{X}_{t+1} \mid \mathbf{X}_t, I_t)$
· Observation model: $p(\mathbf{O}_t \mid \mathbf{X}_t)$
· Belief state: $b_t = p(\mathbf{X}_t \mid \mathcal{O}_{1:t})$
· Reward: $R(b_t, I_t) = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{X}_t \sim b_t}[\text{API}_{\text{cap}}(\mathbf{A}_t) - \lambda_I C_I(I_t) - \lambda_R R(\mathbf{X}_t, I_t)]$

The RC-POMDP is:

\pi^* = \arg\max_{\pi \in \Pi} \; \mathbb{E}_\pi \left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t R(b_t, I_t) \right]

subject to history-dependent cost constraints for all reachable $b_t$:

V_{c_i}^\pi(b_t) \le \hat{c}_i(b_t), \quad i \in \{\text{safety}, \text{authority}, \text{consent}, \text{ethical}\}

This formulation preserves BPO and admits deterministic optimal policies.

5.3 Relationship to MNIP

The MNIP is the myopic approximation of the long-horizon policy:

I_t^{\text{MNIP}} = \arg\min_{I_t \in \mathcal{I}} \; \big\| I_t - I_t^{\text{needed}} \big\|_{\mathcal{C}}^2

subject to safety, non-decline of $\text{API}_{\text{cap}}$, and upper-bound constraints. The MNIP is optimal only when the value function is separable across time; in general, the long-horizon policy deviates from MNIP when the future value of preserved capability justifies accepting slightly higher current risk.

5.4 Stochastic Policy

\pi_\theta(I_t \mid \hat{\mathbf{X}}_t) = \text{softmax}\left( -\beta \left[ \mathcal{C}(I_t) + \lambda \cdot \text{Risk}(\hat{\mathbf{X}}_t, I_t) \right] \right)

Identification: The product $\alpha_i \cdot \beta$ is what matters for the policy. We fix $\alpha_1 = 1$ as a numeraire and estimate the ratios $\alpha_2/\alpha_1$, $\alpha_3/\alpha_1$, and $\beta$ separately via discrete choice experiments with older adults and caregivers.

5.5 Action Space

I_t = (I_t^{\text{assist}}, I_t^{\text{challenge}}, I_t^{\text{modality}}, I_t^{\text{authority}}) \in \mathcal{U}

with $I_t^{\text{assist}} \in [0,1]$, $I_t^{\text{challenge}} \in [0,1]$, $I_t^{\text{modality}} \in \{\text{visual}, \text{auditory}, \text{haptic}, \text{multimodal}\}$, $I_t^{\text{authority}} \in [0,1]$. The needed intervention:

I_t^{\text{needed}} = \arg\min_{I_t \in \mathcal{U}} \|I_t\| \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \Pr(\text{TaskSuccess} \mid \mathbf{X}_t, I_t) \geq 1 - \epsilon_{\text{task}}

