Chapter 7: Trajectory-Based Decline Detection

7.1 From Point Measurements to Trajectories

Group-Based Trajectory Modeling identifies archetypes (stable, stable-then-decline, continuously-decline). ARCS adopts a hybrid approach: population archetypes provide a prior; individual trajectories $\boldsymbol{\tau}_i$ are fit as deviations. Decline is detected when:

\frac{d}{dt} \hat{\mathbf{A}}_t < -\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\text{decline}}

7.2 Hierarchical Bayesian Thresholds

\boldsymbol{\theta}_i(t) \sim \mathcal{N}\big(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{pop}}, \sigma_i^2\big), \qquad \sigma_i^2 \sim \text{Inv-Gamma}(a, b)

In early deployment, the prior dominates; as data accumulate, the posterior shifts toward the individual.

7.3 Reversibility Windows

