Chapter 10: Ethical, Regulatory, and Legal Architecture

10.1 Fundamental Principle

\boxed{\text{A care robot should increase the human's capabilities and freedom whenever possible, rather than progressively replacing them.}}

10.2 Paternalism Constraint

$\text{RobotAuthority} \leq \text{HumanDelegatedAuthority}$ except in pre-negotiated emergency protocols. $\text{Recommendation} \neq \text{Decision}$ and $\text{Assistance} \neq \text{Control}$.

10.3 Tension Management

Tension Question ARCS Resolution
Safety vs. Autonomy When to intervene against preference? Pre-negotiated emergencies; otherwise preference prevails
Surveillance vs. Privacy Detect decline without intrusion? Edge processing, granular consent, data minimization
Presence vs. Replacement Companion or facilitator? $\text{RobotSocialInteraction} \to \text{HumanSocialParticipation}$

10.4 MDR Classification: Configuration-Dependent

· Physical support (fall prevention, sit-to-stand): likely Class IIb under Rule 10 or 11.
· Cognitive scaffolding without physical intervention: classification depends on intended purpose (diagnostic/therapeutic vs. wellness).
· Longitudinal data for clinical decision support: may fall under both MDR and AI Act's high-risk category.

A formal regulatory analysis with a notified body is required before deployment.

10.5 Liability for Adaptive Systems

· Manufacturer liability for algorithm quality, including safety properties.
· Institutional liability for prescribing and configuring the system.
· No-fault compensation for harms arising from correct execution of an incorrectly specified policy.
· Emergency override protocols pre-negotiated, documented, and periodically reviewed.

10.6 Ethics of Techno-Care

No existing ethical framework adequately copes with the autonomy-related challenges of techno-care. We introduce a formalized ethical objective:

\pi^* = \arg\max_{\pi \in \Pi} \left[ J(\pi) - \lambda_E E_{\text{eth}}(\pi) \right]

where $E_{\text{eth}}$ encodes:

· Minimally-necessary deception: Interventions should not deceive beyond therapeutic necessity.
· Techno-care transparency: Robot capabilities and limitations communicable.
· Dignity preservation: Avoid infantilization.
· Relational autonomy: Autonomy exercised through relationships.

These are formulated as additional cost constraints in the RC-POMDP: $V_{E_j}^\pi(b_t) \le \hat{e}_j(b_t)$, $j = 1, \dots, 4$.

