Chapter 11: Research Hypotheses

H1 — Individual Baseline Hypothesis. A latent trajectory model trained on longitudinal multimodal data detects functional deviations with sensitivity $\geq 80\%$ and specificity $\geq 75\%$, achieving AUC $\geq 0.80$ against a pre-specified reference standard ($\text{MDC}_{95}$) in $N \geq 100$ participants over 12 months. $N \approx 205$ (with 25% attrition: 274).

H2 — Minimum Intervention Hypothesis. A bounded MNIP policy maintains task engagement $\geq 70\%$, compared to $\leq 40\%$ under fixed high assistance, without significant increase in adverse events (non-inferiority margin: 2 falls per 1000 person-days).

H3 — Multidimensional Coupling Hypothesis. A joint model with coupling matrix $\mathbf{W}$ predicts global functional decline with $\text{RMSE}_{\text{joint}} \leq 0.80 \times \text{RMSE}_{\text{independent}}$ in $N \geq 200$ participants over 18 months. $N \approx 251$ (with 25% attrition: 335).

H4 — Longitudinal Adaptation Hypothesis. The closed-loop long-horizon policy slows $\text{API}_{\text{cap}}$ decline by $\geq 30\%$ over 12 months compared to a static policy, with effect size $d \geq 0.3$ in a randomized controlled trial. $N = 352$ per arm (with 25% attrition: 470). Non-inferiority fallback: if cohort data does not support $d \geq 0.2$, redesign as non-inferiority trial.

H5 — Human-Control Hypothesis. Explicit inclusion of $\mathbf{H}_t$ as a state variable increases technology acceptance (Almere) by $\geq 25\%$ and reduces system abandonment by $\geq 40\%$ at 6 months.

