Appendix C — Identifiability Analysis

C.1 Nonlinear Observability

Consider the augmented state $\mathbf{Z}_t = [\mathbf{X}_t^\top, \mathbf{W}^\top]^\top$. The observation map is $\mathbf{O}_t = H(\mathbf{X}_t) + \boldsymbol{\eta}_t$. The nonlinear observability codistribution is generated by the Lie derivatives:

\mathcal{O} = \text{span}\{dH, dL_f H, dL_f^2 H, \dots\}.

Condition (O). The system is locally weakly observable on the reachable set $\mathcal{R}$ if $\text{rank}(\mathcal{O}(\mathbf{X})) = n$ for all $\mathbf{X} \in \mathcal{R}$.

C.2 Fisher Information Matrix for $\mathbf{W}$

Consider the linearized transition model:

\mathbf{X}_{t+1} = F \mathbf{X}_t + \mathbf{W} g(\mathbf{X}_t) + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t, \quad \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\epsilon).

The FIM for $\mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 4}$ (16 parameters) has block entries:

[\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W})]_{(i,j),(k,l)} = [\Sigma_\epsilon^{-1}]_{ik} \sum_t \mathbb{E}[g_j(\mathbf{X}_t) g_l(\mathbf{X}_t)].

Condition (I). $\mathbf{W}$ is locally identifiable if and only if $\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W}) \succ 0$.

C.3 Persistent Excitation

Definition (PE). The regressor sequence $\{g(\mathbf{X}_t)\}$ is persistently exciting of order 4 if there exist $T_0 > 0$, $\alpha_0 > 0$ such that for all $t \ge 0$:

\sum_{\tau=t}^{t+T_0} g(\mathbf{X}_\tau) g(\mathbf{X}_\tau)^\top \succeq \alpha_0 I_4.

Proposition C.1. If $\{g(\mathbf{X}_t)\}$ satisfies PE of order 4 and $\Sigma_\epsilon \succ 0$, then $\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W}) \succ 0$.

Proof. The FIM is a sum of outer products weighted by $\Sigma_\epsilon^{-1} \succ 0$. PE guarantees the sum is positive definite in every parameter direction. This is the classical result (Ljung, 1999, Theorem 2.4). $\blacksquare$

Corollary C.2 (Passive Monitoring Failure). Under a passive care regime where $\mathbf{X}_t$ evolves in a low-dimensional invariant manifold $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathbb{R}^4$ with $\dim \mathcal{M} < 4$, the regressor sequence is rank-deficient, PE fails, and $\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W})$ is singular in directions orthogonal to $\mathcal{M}$.

Corollary C.3 (Active Probing Guarantee). If $\pi$ applies excitation $I_t$ such that the induced state trajectory satisfies PE of order 4, then $\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W}) \succ 0$ and $\mathbf{W}$ is locally identifiable.

C.4 Partial Identification Bounds

When PE fails, $\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W})$ has null space $\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{I})$. Partition $\mathbf{W} = \mathbf{W}_\parallel + \mathbf{W}_\perp$ with $\mathbf{W}_\perp \in \mathcal{N}(\mathcal{I})$. The partial identification set is

\mathcal{W}_{\text{PI}} = \left\{\mathbf{W} : \|\mathbf{W} - \hat{\mathbf{W}}\|_{\mathcal{I}}^2 \le \chi^2_{1-\alpha}(16)\right\}.

The projection onto $\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{I})$ is unbounded; the projection onto the identifiable subspace is a bounded ellipsoid. The identifiable subspace is pre-registered via SVD of the empirical $\sum_\tau g_\tau g_\tau^\top$.

