Appendix D — Dual-Control Probing

D.1 Information Gain Derivation

For Gaussian belief $p(\mathbf{W} \mid \mathcal{O}_{1:t}) = \mathcal{N}(\hat{\mathbf{W}}_t, \Sigma_{\mathbf{W},t})$ and linear observation $\mathbf{O}_{t+1} = \mathbf{W} g(\mathbf{X}_t) + \boldsymbol{\eta}$, $\boldsymbol{\eta} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_o)$:

\mathbb{I}(\mathbf{W}; \mathbf{O}_{t+1} \mid \mathcal{O}_{1:t}, I_t) = \frac{1}{2} \log \det\left(I + \Sigma_{\mathbf{W},t}^{1/2} \mathcal{I}_{t+1}(I_t) \Sigma_{\mathbf{W},t}^{1/2}\right),

where $\mathcal{I}_{t+1} = g g^\top \otimes \Sigma_o^{-1}$. This is Bayesian experimental design: maximizing the expected information gain minimizes the posterior entropy.

D.2 Instantaneous Excitation Bound

Let $R_{\max}$ be the safety budget and $L_R$ the Lipschitz constant of risk in action. Safe probing requires

\|I_t - I_t^{\text{MNIP}}\| \le \frac{R_{\max} - R(\mathbf{X}_t, I_t^{\text{MNIP}})}{L_R}.

Proposition D.1 (Safety of Dual-Control Probing). Under the instantaneous excitation bound, the dual-control policy satisfies $R(\mathbf{X}_t, I_t^\pi) \le R_{\max}$ for all $t$.

Proof. By Lipschitz continuity of $R$ in $I$:

R(\mathbf{X}_t, I_t^\pi) \le R(\mathbf{X}_t, I_t^{\text{MNIP}}) + L_R \|I_t^\pi - I_t^{\text{MNIP}}\| \le R_{\max}. \quad \blacksquare

D.3 Cumulative Excitation Budget

To prevent cumulative physiological harm from repeated probing, we impose

\sum_{t=0}^{T} \eta_t \le \eta_{\text{total}},

where $\eta_t$ is the information-seeking perturbation magnitude at time $t$, and $\eta_{\text{total}}$ is calibrated to the individual's physiological reserve:

\eta_{\text{total}} = c_\eta \cdot \text{SPPB},

with $c_\eta$ a pre-registered constant. For SPPB $\in [0, 12]$, $\eta_{\text{total}} \in [0, 12 c_\eta]$. Passive identifiability through natural daily variation is the first-line strategy; active probing is reserved for cases where passive identification fails after a pre-registered observation period (e.g., 90 days).

