Appendix J — Power Analysis Details

J.1 Sample Size for H1 (Detection)

Under $\text{Se} = 0.80$, $\text{Sp} = 0.75$, $\pi_{\text{prev}} = 0.30$, target half-width $w = 0.10$:

N = \frac{z_{1-\alpha/2}^2 \cdot \text{Se}(1-\text{Se})}{w^2 \cdot \pi_{\text{prev}}} = \frac{1.96^2 \cdot 0.16}{0.01 \cdot 0.30} \approx 205.

With 25% attrition, $N = 274$.

J.2 Sample Size for H3 (Coupling)

Under $\text{RMSE}_{\text{joint}} = 0.80 \times \text{RMSE}_{\text{independent}}$, $d = 0.25$:

N = \frac{2(z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta})^2}{d^2} = \frac{2(1.96 + 0.84)^2}{0.0625} \approx 251.

With 25% attrition, $N = 335$.

J.3 Sample Size for H4 (RCT)

Under $d = 0.30$, $N = 352$ per arm, $N = 1056$ total, inflated to 470 per arm for attrition.

Non-inferiority fallback: If cohort data (ELSA, SHARE) does not support $d \ge 0.2$, redesign as a non-inferiority trial against standard care, with superiority as a secondary endpoint. Pre-register an MCID of 10% slowing of decline.

J.4 Adaptive Interim Analysis

Two interim analyses at $N/3$ and $2N/3$ with O'Brien–Fleming boundaries $\alpha_1 = 0.005$, $\alpha_2 = 0.014$, $\alpha_3 = 0.045$ preserve overall type I error at 0.05. Stopping rules: futility (conditional power $< 0.20$) and efficacy (crossing upper boundary).

