Appendix L — Ethics of Techno-Care

L.1 The Gap in Existing Frameworks

No existing ethical framework adequately copes with the autonomy-related and other challenges of techno-care. The consent state machine (Appendix I) addresses procedural consent but not the deeper ethical challenges of AI care robots.

L.2 Proposed Ethical Layer

The intervention policy $\pi$ is constrained not only by safety and consent but also by a formalized ethical objective $E_{\text{eth}}$:

\pi^* = \arg\max_{\pi \in \Pi} \left[ J(\pi) - \lambda_E E_{\text{eth}}(\pi) \right],

where $E_{\text{eth}}$ encodes:

· Minimally-necessary deception: Interventions should not deceive the user beyond what is therapeutically necessary.
· Techno-care transparency: The robot's capabilities and limitations should be communicable to the user and caregivers.
· Dignity preservation: Interventions should respect the user's dignity, avoiding infantilization.
· Relational autonomy: The user's autonomy is relational, exercised through relationships with caregivers and the robot.

L.3 Constraint Formulation

The ethical constraints are formulated as additional cost constraints in the RC-POMDP:

V_{E_j}^\pi(b_t) \le \hat{e}_j(b_t), \quad j = 1, \dots, 4,

for each ethical principle. These are history-dependent and evaluated recursively, consistent with the RC-POMDP framework.

