Appendix N — Multi-State Survival Model

N.1 State Space

\mathcal{S}_{\text{states}} = \{\text{independent}, \text{community-dwelling with assistance}, \text{institutionalized}, \text{deceased}\}

N.2 Transition Rates

Let $\pi_{jk}(t)$ denote the transition rate from state $j$ to state $k$ at time $t$. The occupancy probability $S_j(t)$ evolves according to the Kolmogorov forward equations:

\frac{dS_j(t)}{dt} = \sum_{k \neq j} \pi_{kj}(t) S_k(t) - S_j(t) \sum_{k \neq j} \pi_{jk}(t).

N.3 Value Functional

J(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi \left[ \int_0^\infty \sum_{j \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{states}}} \omega_j S_j(t) e^{-\gamma t} \left( \text{API}_{\text{cap}}(\mathbf{A}_t) - \lambda_I C_I(I_t) - \lambda_R R(\mathbf{X}_t, I_t) \right) dt \right],

where $\omega_j$ are preference-elicited weights over states.

N.4 Boundedness

By Lemma 4, with $\omega_{\max} = \max_j \omega_j$ and $h_{\min} = \min_j h_j^{\min}$:

|J(\pi)| \le \frac{\omega_{\max} R_{\max}}{\gamma + h_{\min}}.

N.5 Perverse-Incentive Prevention

The multi-state formulation prevents perverse incentives: interventions that preserve community dwelling at the cost of functional autonomy are penalized by the $\text{API}_{\text{cap}}$ term, which is weighted equally across states. The binary formulation, by contrast, would score such interventions favorably.

---

Appendix O — Medical State Vector

O.1 Definition

\mathbf{M}_t = [\text{comorbidity index}, \text{polypharmacy count}, \text{recent hospitalization indicator}]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{n_M}.

O.2 Role in the Dynamics

The medical state enters as an exogenous modulator:

\mathbf{A}_{t+1} = \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{A}_t, I_t, \mathbf{E}_t, \mathbf{M}_t) + \mathbf{W} g(\mathbf{A}_t) + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t.

It does not have its own dynamics within the ARCS model (it is updated from external clinical records), but it strongly modulates the coupling matrix $\mathbf{W}$ and the drift $f$.

O.3 Estimation

$\mathbf{M}_t$ is obtained from electronic health records (EHR) or caregiver-reported data, updated at clinical visits. In the absence of EHR access, it is imputed from sensor-derived proxies (e.g., medication adherence patterns, gait variability).

